&lt;h2&gt;


  Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duplicati&lt;/strong&gt; runs as a web server with a browser-based interface. You configure backup jobs through the UI — select source directories, choose a destination, set encryption, define schedule. Everything is managed through the web interface, including restores. Under the hood, Duplicati splits files into blocks, deduplicates, compresses, encrypts, and uploads to your chosen backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BorgBackup&lt;/strong&gt; is a command-line tool. You initialize a repository, then run &lt;code&gt;borg create&lt;/code&gt; to make archives. There's no daemon, no web UI, no built-in scheduler. For automated backups, wrap Borg in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/apps/borgmatic/"&gt;Borgmatic&lt;/a&gt; (a configuration-driven wrapper) or plain cron jobs. Borg's strength is its deduplication algorithm — content-defined chunking achieves better dedup ratios than Duplicati's fixed-block approach for most workloads.&lt;/p&gt;

